WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:01.920
Okay, so this brings us to module four.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.140
And this is the cross ecosystem contrast comparison

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.380
where we are looking at our different data,

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.660
lung data sets related from HubMap,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.160
the single nucleates that we then process

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.020
into the pseudobulk for this comparison,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.000
as well as the GTECS bulk values

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.440
that we specifically have been,

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.320
so that you have your appropriate age comparison,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.540
and then the geofibrosis cohort,

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.740
as well as the NASA space flight study for mouse.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.220
So, you know, and there's somebody asked like,

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.360
why space flight?

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.120
So the reason space flight is here

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.280
is a comparison about aging and disease

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.700
and it's not necessarily arbitrary.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.740
So microgravity and space radiation

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.260
are studied explicitly as a model of accelerated aging,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.360
producing the inflammation, senescence

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.700
and fibrotic remodeling signatures

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.220
that characterize age tissue

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.120
and even a genomic radiation age association

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.760
has directly been carried into lung diseases.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.239
So this is why it's important for,

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.560
just as an exploratory analysis for this cross ecosystem

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.340
to compare the different age contrasts,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.120
specifically to the fibrosis cohort

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.400
as well as the space flight.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.820
So how do you do that?

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.520
So you have different platforms, different units,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.640
different species even, so different processing.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.320
And this model really talks about the best way to do that

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.100
is to look at what actually does travel.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.020
And it's the contrast.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.420
So within each resource you've computed under one design,

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:34.440
you have the log-to-fold change,

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.060
and then the pathway enrichment score.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.080
So really the normalized enrichment score

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.280
is going to be what we use to be able to find

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.280
concordance between the different data sets

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.620
and the relevant biological mechanisms.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:52.960
So, you know, following again a similar template

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.380
as the previous modules,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.500
looking at our method section.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:01:59.480
We have six steps.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.700
So we have the four within resource contrast,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.480
one, differential expressed path,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.160
and explicit reference levels.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.220
And so all of this to be able to integrate

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.420
at the pathway normalize enrichment score,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.500
and then looked at the share mechanism

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.580
concordance across those different data sets.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.960
So following the same structure, again,

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.420
we go through the calibration of the space flight.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.360
So this is important.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.280
So we looked at specific X-fold spike and calibration

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.100
to ensure that there's legitimacy to the--

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:45.060
there's not any sort of ERCC confounding data problems

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:45.720
or whatsoever.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.120
So that was ran and was successful.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.740
So then we-- important here, critical to address

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.920
is the ortholog mapping.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.940
So we are able to then map the mouse to human tissue

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.460
because again, the NAS-OSDR data is wrote in research data

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.900
and needs to be mapped for proper comparison.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.320
And then it goes into the pathway enrichment values.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:19.160
So we then look at some of our results where, first,

